prompt:
Use Three.js to implement a 3D bouncing ball collision simulation scene.

[Three.js Import Method (Must Be Followed Strictly)]
Use the following importmap in <head>, and import via ES Modules in <script type="module">:
<script type="importmap">
{ "imports": {
    "three": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.170.0/build/three.module.js",
    "three/addons/": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.170.0/examples/jsm/"
} }
</script>
<script type="module">
import * as THREE from 'three';
import { OrbitControls } from 'three/addons/controls/OrbitControls.js';
// Write your code here
</script>

[Functional Requirements]
(1) The scene must contain a 20×20 ground plane and four transparent enclosing walls, forming a closed box;
(2) Initially, there must be 5 spheres of different colors (radius 0.5), starting from random positions with random initial velocities;
(3) The spheres must be affected by gravity (g = 15 m/s², downward) and bounce after colliding with the ground or walls (coefficient of restitution restitution = 0.75);
(4) When spheres collide with each other, apply the correct elastic collision response (equal-mass spheres exchange their normal velocity components);
(5) For every collision (sphere-wall or sphere-sphere), increment the global collision counter by 1;
(6) Clicking anywhere in the scene must generate a new sphere 5m above the clicked position (maximum 30 spheres);
(7) The HUD in the upper-left corner must display in real time: the current number of spheres (id="ballCount") and the total number of collisions (id="collisionCount");
(8) Support mouse-drag camera rotation (OrbitControls);
(9) Each sphere must have a short-lived motion trail behind it;
(10) On every frame, expose the scene state through the window.__3D_STATE__ object, containing the following fields: ballCount (current number of spheres), collisionCount (total number of collisions), balls (an array in which each item contains x/y/z position and vx/vy/vz velocity), and totalKineticEnergy (the sum of the kinetic energy of all spheres = Σ 0.5*v²).

Output a single HTML file that can be opened directly in a browser and run.
